WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.820
Dig up a soybean plant and look at its roots.

2
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.040
You find small round bumps.

3
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.800
Cut one open, and an active one is often pink inside.

4
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:14.220
These bumps are packed with bacteria.

5
00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:18.500
Most of us learn the word bacteria next to the word germs.

6
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.580
So, are bacteria and viruses always harmful?

7
00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:26.013
Living things use nitrogen to build proteins and DNA.

8
00:00:27.093 --> 00:00:28.913
The air is mostly nitrogen gas.

9
00:00:29.873 --> 00:00:32.253
Plants and animals cannot use it in that form.

10
00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:37.873
Nitrogen fixation changes nitrogen gas into a form living things can use.

11
00:00:38.753 --> 00:00:41.073
Only some bacteria and archaea can do this.

12
00:00:41.967 --> 00:00:45.207
In soybeans, these bacteria live inside a nodule.

13
00:00:46.027 --> 00:00:47.947
A nodule is a small swelling on a root.

14
00:00:48.747 --> 00:00:52.867
The bacteria turn nitrogen gas into ammonium, and the plant uses it.

15
00:00:53.587 --> 00:00:57.207
The plant sends the bacteria energy-rich food from photosynthesis.

16
00:00:58.187 --> 00:01:00.887
When both species benefit, we call it mutualism.

17
00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:04.047
Now picture a stream full of fallen leaves.

18
00:01:04.927 --> 00:01:08.307
A decomposer breaks down dead plants, animals and wastes.

19
00:01:09.307 --> 00:01:11.347
Bacteria and fungi do most of this work.

20
00:01:12.147 --> 00:01:15.787
They release enzymes that break large molecules into smaller ones.

21
00:01:16.647 --> 00:01:18.707
The nutrients return to the soil or water.

22
00:01:19.627 --> 00:01:24.247
Without decomposers, dead matter would pile up, and its nutrients would stay locked

23
00:01:24.247 --> 00:01:25.247
inside.

24
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.860
Your gut holds trillions of bacteria.

25
00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:30.600
This community is your microbiome.

26
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.040
Gut bacteria break down fiber by fermentation, which works without oxygen.

27
00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:40.740
A pathogen is a microbe or virus that causes disease in its host.

28
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.840
The host is the living thing it lives in or on.

29
00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:48.620
Most strains of E. coli live quietly in your large intestine.

30
00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:54.180
Other strains carry extra genes that make them harmful, and they cause illness when

31
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.220
people swallow them in food or water.

32
00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:58.800
Viruses matter too.

33
00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:02.340
A bacteriophage is a virus that infects bacteria.

34
00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:07.360
In a lab model ecosystem, a virus infected an ocean alga.

35
00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.240
Bacteria then broke down the dead algae.

36
00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.220
The water gained nitrogen and phosphorus that other living things could use.

37
00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.400
So, are bacteria and viruses always harmful?

38
00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.680
No. Whether a microbe helps or harms depends on which microbe it is, and where it

39
00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:24.880
lives.
